window.MARGIN_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"g":["garden"],"h":["Ideas"],"t":"Ideas","u":"garden\/ideas.html","x":"Ideas\nLoose threads that might become essays.\nA reading log that only records the questions a book raised.\nMaps of walks drawn from memory, compared with the real route.\nLetters to a future self, published only after a year. See Reading List.\nA private list of drafts — kept in Journal 2026-09-20, which is not published."},{"g":[],"h":["Reading List"],"t":"Reading List","u":"garden\/reading-list.html","x":"Reading List\nThe Craft of Notes (synthetic title)\nMargins and Marginalia (synthetic title)\nA Year of Small Writing (synthetic title)\nBack to Ideas."},{"g":["demo","start-here"],"h":["Welcome to the Margin demo","What's here","Things to try"],"t":"Welcome to the Margin demo","u":"index.html","x":"Welcome to the Margin demo\nThis folder is entirely synthetic. It exists to show how Margin edits ordinary Markdown files without importing them anywhere.\nTry this\nMove the caret onto a line with formatting. The Markdown characters appear so you can edit them, and fade away again when you move on.What's here\nField Notes — a longer piece with several headings (open the outline with ⌃⌘I).\nStyle Guide — tables, code, and quotes.\nIdeas and Reading List in the garden.\nCardamom Buns, because every notebook needs a recipe.\nA synthetic Plume transcript in Transcripts\/.\nThings to try\nOpen this folder with File ▸ Open Folder…\nClick a checkbox to toggle it\nPress ⌘\/ to switch between Live and Source\nPublish a few notes with File ▸ Publish Site…\nPrivate notes such as the journal stay private unless you check them in the publish window. Links to them from published pages become plain text.\nWritten with ==care== and #markdown."},{"g":["recipe"],"h":["Cardamom Buns","Dough","Method"],"t":"Cardamom Buns","u":"kitchen\/cardamom-buns.html","x":"Cardamom Buns\nMakes 12. A synthetic recipe for demonstration.\nDough\nIngredient Amount Flour 500 g \nMilk, warm 250 ml \nButter, soft 75 g \nSugar 60 g \nYeast 7 g \nGround cardamom 2 tsp \nMethod\nMix everything into a soft dough and knead for 8 minutes.\nRise until doubled, about an hour.\nRoll out, spread with cardamom butter, fold, cut and twist.\nBake at 200 °C for 12–14 minutes.\nTiming\nThe second rise takes about 30 minutes in a warm kitchen."},{"g":["writing","tools"],"h":["Field Notes on Quiet Tools","Plain files outlast apps","Formatting as you type","Where the markup goes","Publishing without a platform","Small rituals"],"t":"Field Notes on Quiet Tools","u":"writing\/field-notes.html","x":"Field Notes on Quiet Tools\nSome tools ask for attention; others give it back. A good notebook is the second kind. These notes collect small observations about software that stays out of the way.\nPlain files outlast apps\nA folder of Markdown files can be read by almost anything: a text editor, a script, a static site generator, a future program nobody has written yet. When the file is the note, there is nothing to export and nothing to migrate.\nThat is also why an editor should never quietly rewrite a file just because it was opened. Line endings, byte-order marks and odd bits of syntax belong to the author.\nFormatting as you type\nLive formatting works best when it is honest. Headings look like headings and bold looks bold, but the characters that make them are still there, one caret-movement away. Nothing is converted behind your back.\nWhere the markup goes\nWhen the caret leaves a line, the markers fade into the margin. Return to it and they come back. The text on disk never changes.\nPublishing without a platform\nA personal site can be a folder of static pages. Choose the notes that should be public; everything else stays on your machine. Links from a public note to a private one simply become text, so nothing leaks by accident. See the Style Guide for how the pages look.\nSmall rituals\nWrite the title last.\nKeep one idea per note.\nLink generously, publish sparingly.\nThe margin is where the reader talks back."},{"g":[],"h":["Style Guide","Inline","Lists","Table","Code","Quote","Image"],"t":"Style Guide","u":"writing\/style-guide.html","x":"Style Guide\nA reference page that exercises the formatting Margin understands.\nInline\nText can be strong, emphasized, both, struck, ==highlighted==, or inline code. Links go outward or back home. Tags look like #reference.\nLists\nBullets nest\nlike this\nTasks can be open\nor done\nOrdered\nLists\nCount\nTable\nElement Syntax Published as Heading ## Title <h2> \nTask - [ ] checkbox \nWiki link [[Note]] link or plain text \nCode\nstruct Note {\n    var title: String\n    var body: String\n}\n\nQuote\nUnsupported syntax, like footnotes[^1] or $inline math$, stays exactly as typed.\nImage\nA sketch of the margin"}];
